# Pension / Retirement System Benchmark Comparison — Claude Skill

A reusable [Claude Skill](https://support.claude.com/en/articles/12512176-what-are-skills) that automates comparing a public pension or retirement system's funding, investment, cost, and governance metrics against national and state/regional peer benchmarks.

## What it does

- Gathers a plan's own primary documents (actuarial valuation, audited financial statements, investment performance report)
- Finds the right **national** benchmark (e.g. NCPERS's annual Public Retirement Systems Study for U.S. public plans) and the right **state/regional** benchmark (e.g. a state pension oversight office's aggregated statewide data)
- Cross-checks numbers across sources to catch extraction/transcription errors before relying on them
- Computes and explains the comparison — funded ratio (on multiple bases where available), discount rate, investment returns, asset allocation, costs, contribution rates, amortization method/period, COLA policy
- Returns a clean **chat summary**, not an auto-generated file — you decide how and whether to turn it into a doc, deck, or memo

## Install

1. Download `pension-benchmark-comparison.skill` from this repo's [Releases](../../releases) page.
2. In Claude, go to **Settings → Capabilities (Skills) → Upload skill**, and select the downloaded file.
3. Make sure code execution is enabled — the skill requires it.

## Use

Just describe your comparison task in chat, for example:

> "Compare the [City] Police & Fire pension plan against NCPERS national data and our state's pension oversight database. Here are the plan's latest actuarial valuation, financial statement, and investment report: [links or uploads]."

Claude will locate the benchmark sources, cross-check figures against the plan's own documents, and hand back a structured comparison in the conversation.

To refresh a comparison with a newer quarterly report or valuation, just point Claude at the new documents — no need to redo the setup.

## Notes

- Built and tested on a real municipal police & fire pension board's comparison against NCPERS and a state pension oversight database. Generalized so it works for any public pension plan, any state or country, and whichever national benchmark fits that plan's category.
- This is not investment, actuarial, or legal advice. The skill organizes and compares publicly available figures — always verify against primary sources before relying on the output in an official capacity.
- Built on Anthropic's open [Agent Skills](https://agentskills.io) standard.

